% LINK and LINK_LEFT in the SQL lane: the joined row, the binders' scope, and
% what a join lets the planner push down. Spec §7.4 "Joined rows" and "How a
% LINK evaluates".

### name: link.row.is-the-promoted-fields
--- note
A joined row in SQL is its promoted fields (spec §7.4 "Joined rows"): each
side's fields whose names the other side does not have -- `id` and `name`
are on both sides, so neither is a column of the row. The binders (`_1`,
`_2`, ORDERS, O) are nested records SQL has no column for. This used to
render as `SELECT o.*`, the left table's columns, which is a row SEL never
produces.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"ORDERS": {"kind": "relation", "from": "orders", "alias": "o", "fields": {"ID": {"table": "o", "column": "id", "type": "NUM"}, "CUSTOMER_ID": {"table": "o", "column": "customer_id", "type": "NUM"}, "AMOUNT": {"table": "o", "column": "amount", "type": "NUM"}, "NAME": {"table": "o", "column": "name", "type": "TEXT"}}},
 "CUSTOMERS": {"kind": "relation", "from": "customers", "alias": "c", "fields": {"ID": {"table": "c", "column": "id", "type": "NUM"}, "NAME": {"table": "c", "column": "name", "type": "TEXT"}}}}
--- as
statement
--- source
ORDERS .> LINK(CUSTOMERS, _1["customer_id"] == _2["id"])
--- expect
SELECT `o`.`customer_id`, `o`.`amount` FROM `orders` `o` INNER JOIN `customers` `c` ON (`o`.`customer_id` = `c`.`id`)
===
### name: link.row.filter-on-a-promoted-field
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"ORDERS": {"kind": "relation", "from": "orders", "alias": "o", "fields": {"ID": {"table": "o", "column": "id", "type": "NUM"}, "CUSTOMER_ID": {"table": "o", "column": "customer_id", "type": "NUM"}, "AMOUNT": {"table": "o", "column": "amount", "type": "NUM"}, "NAME": {"table": "o", "column": "name", "type": "TEXT"}}},
 "CUSTOMERS": {"kind": "relation", "from": "customers", "alias": "c", "fields": {"ID": {"table": "c", "column": "id", "type": "NUM"}, "NAME": {"table": "c", "column": "name", "type": "TEXT"}}}}
--- as
statement
--- source
ORDERS .> LINK(CUSTOMERS, O, C, O["customer_id"] == C["id"]) .> FILTER(_["amount"] > 5)
--- expect
SELECT `o`.`customer_id`, `o`.`amount` FROM `orders` `o` INNER JOIN `customers` `c` ON (`o`.`customer_id` = `c`.`id`) WHERE (`o`.`amount` > 5)
===
### name: link.row.a-name-both-sides-have-is-refused
--- note
`name` is on both sides, so the joined row has no `name`: `run()` raises
E_NO_KEY at the read, and the translator refuses at the same place. C++
once resolved it to the left side's column.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"ORDERS": {"kind": "relation", "from": "orders", "alias": "o", "fields": {"ID": {"table": "o", "column": "id", "type": "NUM"}, "CUSTOMER_ID": {"table": "o", "column": "customer_id", "type": "NUM"}, "AMOUNT": {"table": "o", "column": "amount", "type": "NUM"}, "NAME": {"table": "o", "column": "name", "type": "TEXT"}}},
 "CUSTOMERS": {"kind": "relation", "from": "customers", "alias": "c", "fields": {"ID": {"table": "c", "column": "id", "type": "NUM"}, "NAME": {"table": "c", "column": "name", "type": "TEXT"}}}}
--- as
statement
--- source
ORDERS .> LINK(CUSTOMERS, _1["customer_id"] == _2["id"]) .> MAP(RECORD("name", _["name"]))
--- error
E_SQL_SHAPE 1:81
===
### name: link.row.select-cols-of-a-shared-name-is-refused
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"ORDERS": {"kind": "relation", "from": "orders", "alias": "o", "fields": {"ID": {"table": "o", "column": "id", "type": "NUM"}, "CUSTOMER_ID": {"table": "o", "column": "customer_id", "type": "NUM"}, "AMOUNT": {"table": "o", "column": "amount", "type": "NUM"}, "NAME": {"table": "o", "column": "name", "type": "TEXT"}}},
 "CUSTOMERS": {"kind": "relation", "from": "customers", "alias": "c", "fields": {"ID": {"table": "c", "column": "id", "type": "NUM"}, "NAME": {"table": "c", "column": "name", "type": "TEXT"}}}}
--- as
statement
--- source
ORDERS .> LINK(CUSTOMERS, _1["customer_id"] == _2["id"]) .> SELECT_COLS("id")
--- error
E_SQL_SHAPE 1:73
===
### name: link.row.a-named-binder-is-the-same-row
--- note
A step's own binder after the join is the joined row under another name:
the same fields, the same refusal of a name both sides have. Every host
resolved `r["name"]` to the left side because the ambiguity check was
gated on the binder being `_`.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"ORDERS": {"kind": "relation", "from": "orders", "alias": "o", "fields": {"ID": {"table": "o", "column": "id", "type": "NUM"}, "CUSTOMER_ID": {"table": "o", "column": "customer_id", "type": "NUM"}, "AMOUNT": {"table": "o", "column": "amount", "type": "NUM"}, "NAME": {"table": "o", "column": "name", "type": "TEXT"}}},
 "CUSTOMERS": {"kind": "relation", "from": "customers", "alias": "c", "fields": {"ID": {"table": "c", "column": "id", "type": "NUM"}, "NAME": {"table": "c", "column": "name", "type": "TEXT"}}}}
--- as
statement
--- source
ORDERS .> LINK(CUSTOMERS, _1["customer_id"] == _2["id"]) .> MAP(r, RECORD("name", r["name"]))
--- error
E_SQL_SHAPE 1:84
===
### name: link.row.a-named-binder-reads-a-promoted-field
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"ORDERS": {"kind": "relation", "from": "orders", "alias": "o", "fields": {"ID": {"table": "o", "column": "id", "type": "NUM"}, "CUSTOMER_ID": {"table": "o", "column": "customer_id", "type": "NUM"}, "AMOUNT": {"table": "o", "column": "amount", "type": "NUM"}, "NAME": {"table": "o", "column": "name", "type": "TEXT"}}},
 "CUSTOMERS": {"kind": "relation", "from": "customers", "alias": "c", "fields": {"ID": {"table": "c", "column": "id", "type": "NUM"}, "NAME": {"table": "c", "column": "name", "type": "TEXT"}}}}
--- as
statement
--- source
ORDERS .> LINK(CUSTOMERS, _1["customer_id"] == _2["id"]) .> MAP(r, RECORD("a", r["amount"]))
--- expect
SELECT `o`.`amount` AS `a` FROM `orders` `o` INNER JOIN `customers` `c` ON (`o`.`customer_id` = `c`.`id`)
===

### name: link.binders.left-binder-does-not-outlive-the-link
--- note
The binders are scoped to the predicate (spec §7.4): after the LINK the row
carries them as keys, and `O` is not a name -- `run()` raises E_UNDEF_VAR.
Three hosts refused `O` and accepted `C`, two accepted both (finding W2,
d2); every host now refuses every binder after the LINK.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"ORDERS": {"kind": "relation", "from": "orders", "alias": "o", "fields": {"ID": {"table": "o", "column": "id", "type": "NUM"}, "CUSTOMER_ID": {"table": "o", "column": "customer_id", "type": "NUM"}, "AMOUNT": {"table": "o", "column": "amount", "type": "NUM"}, "NAME": {"table": "o", "column": "name", "type": "TEXT"}}},
 "CUSTOMERS": {"kind": "relation", "from": "customers", "alias": "c", "fields": {"ID": {"table": "c", "column": "id", "type": "NUM"}, "NAME": {"table": "c", "column": "name", "type": "TEXT"}}}}
--- as
statement
--- source
ORDERS .> LINK(CUSTOMERS, O, C, O["customer_id"] == C["id"]) .> FILTER(O["id"] > 1)
--- error
E_SQL_UNBOUND 1:72
===
### name: link.binders.right-binder-does-not-outlive-the-link
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"ORDERS": {"kind": "relation", "from": "orders", "alias": "o", "fields": {"ID": {"table": "o", "column": "id", "type": "NUM"}, "CUSTOMER_ID": {"table": "o", "column": "customer_id", "type": "NUM"}, "AMOUNT": {"table": "o", "column": "amount", "type": "NUM"}, "NAME": {"table": "o", "column": "name", "type": "TEXT"}}},
 "CUSTOMERS": {"kind": "relation", "from": "customers", "alias": "c", "fields": {"ID": {"table": "c", "column": "id", "type": "NUM"}, "NAME": {"table": "c", "column": "name", "type": "TEXT"}}}}
--- as
statement
--- source
ORDERS .> LINK(CUSTOMERS, O, C, O["customer_id"] == C["id"]) .> FILTER(C["id"] > 1)
--- error
E_SQL_UNBOUND 1:72
===
### name: link.binders.positional-binder-does-not-outlive-the-link
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"ORDERS": {"kind": "relation", "from": "orders", "alias": "o", "fields": {"ID": {"table": "o", "column": "id", "type": "NUM"}, "CUSTOMER_ID": {"table": "o", "column": "customer_id", "type": "NUM"}, "AMOUNT": {"table": "o", "column": "amount", "type": "NUM"}, "NAME": {"table": "o", "column": "name", "type": "TEXT"}}},
 "CUSTOMERS": {"kind": "relation", "from": "customers", "alias": "c", "fields": {"ID": {"table": "c", "column": "id", "type": "NUM"}, "NAME": {"table": "c", "column": "name", "type": "TEXT"}}}}
--- as
statement
--- source
ORDERS .> LINK(CUSTOMERS, O, C, O["customer_id"] == C["id"]) .> FILTER(_2["id"] > 1)
--- error
E_SQL_UNBOUND 1:72
===
### name: link.binders.map-of-a-binder-is-refused
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"ORDERS": {"kind": "relation", "from": "orders", "alias": "o", "fields": {"ID": {"table": "o", "column": "id", "type": "NUM"}, "CUSTOMER_ID": {"table": "o", "column": "customer_id", "type": "NUM"}, "AMOUNT": {"table": "o", "column": "amount", "type": "NUM"}, "NAME": {"table": "o", "column": "name", "type": "TEXT"}}},
 "CUSTOMERS": {"kind": "relation", "from": "customers", "alias": "c", "fields": {"ID": {"table": "c", "column": "id", "type": "NUM"}, "NAME": {"table": "c", "column": "name", "type": "TEXT"}}}}
--- as
statement
--- source
ORDERS .> LINK(CUSTOMERS, O, C, O["customer_id"] == C["id"]) .> MAP(O["id"])
--- error
E_SQL_UNBOUND 1:69
===
### name: link.derived.projects-the-promoted-fields
--- note
A derived table over a join carries the joined row's promoted fields, so a
step above it reads what SEL's row has. It used to be `SELECT o.*`, and a
read of the right side's column above it was SQL the database rejected.
--- dialect
sqlite
--- bindings
{"ORDERS": {"kind": "relation", "from": "orders", "alias": "o", "fields": {"ID": {"table": "o", "column": "id", "type": "NUM"}, "CUSTOMER_ID": {"table": "o", "column": "customer_id", "type": "NUM"}, "AMOUNT": {"table": "o", "column": "amount", "type": "NUM"}, "NAME": {"table": "o", "column": "name", "type": "TEXT"}}},
 "CUSTOMERS": {"kind": "relation", "from": "customers", "alias": "c", "fields": {"ID": {"table": "c", "column": "id", "type": "NUM"}, "NAME": {"table": "c", "column": "name", "type": "TEXT"}}}}
--- as
statement
--- source
ORDERS .> LINK(CUSTOMERS, _1["customer_id"] == _2["id"]) .> TAKE(2) .> MAP(RECORD("a", _["amount"]))
--- expect
SELECT "_sub1"."amount" AS "a" FROM (SELECT "o"."customer_id", "o"."amount" FROM "orders" "o" INNER JOIN "customers" "c" ON (CAST("o"."customer_id" AS NUMERIC) = CAST("c"."id" AS NUMERIC)) LIMIT 2) "_sub1"
===
### name: link.derived.a-shared-name-above-it-is-refused
--- dialect
sqlite
--- bindings
{"ORDERS": {"kind": "relation", "from": "orders", "alias": "o", "fields": {"ID": {"table": "o", "column": "id", "type": "NUM"}, "CUSTOMER_ID": {"table": "o", "column": "customer_id", "type": "NUM"}, "AMOUNT": {"table": "o", "column": "amount", "type": "NUM"}, "NAME": {"table": "o", "column": "name", "type": "TEXT"}}},
 "CUSTOMERS": {"kind": "relation", "from": "customers", "alias": "c", "fields": {"ID": {"table": "c", "column": "id", "type": "NUM"}, "NAME": {"table": "c", "column": "name", "type": "TEXT"}}}}
--- as
statement
--- source
ORDERS .> LINK(CUSTOMERS, _1["customer_id"] == _2["id"]) .> TAKE(2) .> MAP(RECORD("n", _["name"]))
--- error
E_SQL_BINDING 1:89
===
### name: plan.link.unprojected-join-is-not-a-split-point
--- note
SQL's rows for a LINK lack the binders SEL's rows carry, so until a MAP, a
SELECT_COLS or a BUCKET makes them exact the prefix is not a split point
and not a full pushdown -- a continuation would read `_["C"]` where the
database sent nothing (finding Y, lanes). `joinRowsLackBinders` beside
`bucketRowsAreKeys` in each planner.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"ORDERS": {"kind": "relation", "from": "orders", "alias": "o", "fields": {"ID": {"table": "o", "column": "id", "type": "NUM"}, "CUSTOMER_ID": {"table": "o", "column": "customer_id", "type": "NUM"}, "AMOUNT": {"table": "o", "column": "amount", "type": "NUM"}, "NAME": {"table": "o", "column": "name", "type": "TEXT"}}},
 "CUSTOMERS": {"kind": "relation", "from": "customers", "alias": "c", "fields": {"ID": {"table": "c", "column": "id", "type": "NUM"}, "NAME": {"table": "c", "column": "name", "type": "TEXT"}}}}
--- source
ORDERS .> LINK(CUSTOMERS, _1["customer_id"] == _2["id"]) .> TAKE(2)
--- plan
pure_memory
--- tables
orders
customers
===
### name: plan.link.projection-closes-the-join
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"ORDERS": {"kind": "relation", "from": "orders", "alias": "o", "fields": {"ID": {"table": "o", "column": "id", "type": "NUM"}, "CUSTOMER_ID": {"table": "o", "column": "customer_id", "type": "NUM"}, "AMOUNT": {"table": "o", "column": "amount", "type": "NUM"}, "NAME": {"table": "o", "column": "name", "type": "TEXT"}}},
 "CUSTOMERS": {"kind": "relation", "from": "customers", "alias": "c", "fields": {"ID": {"table": "c", "column": "id", "type": "NUM"}, "NAME": {"table": "c", "column": "name", "type": "TEXT"}}}}
--- source
ORDERS .> LINK(CUSTOMERS, _1["customer_id"] == _2["id"]) .> MAP(RECORD("a", _["amount"], "s", REPEAT("x", _["customer_id"])))
--- plan
pure_sql
--- tables
orders
customers
--- expect
SELECT `o`.`amount` AS `a`, REPEAT('x', `o`.`customer_id`) AS `s` FROM `orders` `o` INNER JOIN `customers` `c` ON (`o`.`customer_id` = `c`.`id`)
===
### name: plan.link.fall-through-over-a-join-projects-its-dependencies
--- note
The MAP fall-through over a joined prefix is exact: its custom half reads a
promoted field, which the SQL half projects.
--- dialect
sqlite
--- bindings
{"ORDERS": {"kind": "relation", "from": "orders", "alias": "o", "fields": {"ID": {"table": "o", "column": "id", "type": "NUM"}, "CUSTOMER_ID": {"table": "o", "column": "customer_id", "type": "NUM"}, "AMOUNT": {"table": "o", "column": "amount", "type": "NUM"}, "NAME": {"table": "o", "column": "name", "type": "TEXT"}}},
 "CUSTOMERS": {"kind": "relation", "from": "customers", "alias": "c", "fields": {"ID": {"table": "c", "column": "id", "type": "NUM"}, "NAME": {"table": "c", "column": "name", "type": "TEXT"}}}}
--- source
ORDERS .> LINK(CUSTOMERS, _1["customer_id"] == _2["id"]) .> MAP(RECORD("a", _["amount"], "s", REPEAT("x", _["customer_id"])))
--- plan
hybrid
--- tables
orders
customers
--- expect
SELECT "o"."amount" AS "a", "o"."customer_id" AS "customer_id" FROM "orders" "o" INNER JOIN "customers" "c" ON (CAST("o"."customer_id" AS NUMERIC) = CAST("c"."id" AS NUMERIC))
===
### name: plan.link.select-cols-closes-the-join
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"ORDERS": {"kind": "relation", "from": "orders", "alias": "o", "fields": {"ID": {"table": "o", "column": "id", "type": "NUM"}, "CUSTOMER_ID": {"table": "o", "column": "customer_id", "type": "NUM"}, "AMOUNT": {"table": "o", "column": "amount", "type": "NUM"}, "NAME": {"table": "o", "column": "name", "type": "TEXT"}}},
 "CUSTOMERS": {"kind": "relation", "from": "customers", "alias": "c", "fields": {"ID": {"table": "c", "column": "id", "type": "NUM"}, "NAME": {"table": "c", "column": "name", "type": "TEXT"}}}}
--- source
ORDERS .> LINK(CUSTOMERS, _1["customer_id"] == _2["id"]) .> SELECT_COLS("amount")
--- plan
pure_sql
--- tables
orders
customers
--- expect
SELECT `o`.`amount` FROM `orders` `o` INNER JOIN `customers` `c` ON (`o`.`customer_id` = `c`.`id`)
===
### name: plan.link.projected-bucket-closes-the-join
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"ORDERS": {"kind": "relation", "from": "orders", "alias": "o", "fields": {"ID": {"table": "o", "column": "id", "type": "NUM"}, "CUSTOMER_ID": {"table": "o", "column": "customer_id", "type": "NUM"}, "AMOUNT": {"table": "o", "column": "amount", "type": "NUM"}, "NAME": {"table": "o", "column": "name", "type": "TEXT"}}},
 "CUSTOMERS": {"kind": "relation", "from": "customers", "alias": "c", "fields": {"ID": {"table": "c", "column": "id", "type": "NUM"}, "NAME": {"table": "c", "column": "name", "type": "TEXT"}}}}
--- source
ORDERS .> LINK(CUSTOMERS, _1["customer_id"] == _2["id"]) .> BUCKET(_["customer_id"], RECORD("c", _K, "n", COUNT(_)))
--- plan
pure_sql
--- tables
orders
customers
--- expect
SELECT MIN(`o`.`customer_id`) AS `c`, COUNT(*) AS `n` FROM `orders` `o` INNER JOIN `customers` `c` ON (`o`.`customer_id` = `c`.`id`) GROUP BY CAST(`o`.`customer_id` AS CHAR) COLLATE utf8mb4_nopad_bin
===
### name: plan.link.a-binder-after-the-join-stays-in-memory
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"ORDERS": {"kind": "relation", "from": "orders", "alias": "o", "fields": {"ID": {"table": "o", "column": "id", "type": "NUM"}, "CUSTOMER_ID": {"table": "o", "column": "customer_id", "type": "NUM"}, "AMOUNT": {"table": "o", "column": "amount", "type": "NUM"}, "NAME": {"table": "o", "column": "name", "type": "TEXT"}}},
 "CUSTOMERS": {"kind": "relation", "from": "customers", "alias": "c", "fields": {"ID": {"table": "c", "column": "id", "type": "NUM"}, "NAME": {"table": "c", "column": "name", "type": "TEXT"}}}}
--- source
ORDERS .> LINK(CUSTOMERS, O, C, O["customer_id"] == C["id"]) .> FILTER(C["id"] > 1)
--- plan
pure_memory
--- tables
orders
customers
===
### name: link.qualify.fields-without-a-table-take-the-relation-alias
--- note
A field binding may leave `table` out; on its own relation the column renders
bare in every host. Once a join is present the column must say which side it
is on -- two tables may share a name, and the server refuses an ambiguous one
-- so it is qualified by the alias its relation renders under, whatever the
binding declared. Four hosts did; the Lisp host rendered the binding as
written and emitted `name`, `amount` and `ON (customer_id = id)` (review
2026-09-15, low-severity report reproduced 2026-09-22; SEL-0042). The fuzz
corpus binds every field to a table, so it could not see it.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"ORDERS": {"kind": "relation", "from": "orders", "alias": "o", "fields": {"ID": {"column": "id", "type": "NUM"}, "CUSTOMER_ID": {"column": "customer_id", "type": "NUM"}, "AMOUNT": {"column": "amount", "type": "NUM"}}},
 "CUSTOMERS": {"kind": "relation", "from": "customers", "alias": "c", "fields": {"ID": {"column": "id", "type": "NUM"}, "NAME": {"column": "name", "type": "TEXT"}}}}
--- as
statement
--- source
ORDERS .> LINK(CUSTOMERS, _1["CUSTOMER_ID"] == _2["ID"]) .> MAP(RECORD("name", _["NAME"], "amount", _["AMOUNT"]))
--- expect
SELECT `c`.`name` AS `name`, `o`.`amount` AS `amount` FROM `orders` `o` INNER JOIN `customers` `c` ON (`o`.`customer_id` = `c`.`id`)
===
### name: link.qualify.the-table-name-without-an-alias-and-the-joined-side-by-position
--- note
With no alias on either side the left renders under its table name and the
joined side under the positional `_2` every host gives it.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"ORDERS": {"kind": "relation", "from": "orders", "fields": {"ID": {"column": "id", "type": "NUM"}, "CUSTOMER_ID": {"column": "customer_id", "type": "NUM"}, "AMOUNT": {"column": "amount", "type": "NUM"}}},
 "CUSTOMERS": {"kind": "relation", "from": "customers", "fields": {"ID": {"column": "id", "type": "NUM"}, "NAME": {"column": "name", "type": "TEXT"}}}}
--- as
statement
--- source
ORDERS .> LINK(CUSTOMERS, _1["CUSTOMER_ID"] == _2["ID"]) .> MAP(RECORD("name", _["NAME"], "amount", _["AMOUNT"]))
--- expect
SELECT `_2`.`name` AS `name`, `orders`.`amount` AS `amount` FROM `orders` INNER JOIN `customers` `_2` ON (`orders`.`customer_id` = `_2`.`id`)
===
### name: link.qualify.a-named-binder-is-the-same-row
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"ORDERS": {"kind": "relation", "from": "orders", "alias": "o", "fields": {"ID": {"column": "id", "type": "NUM"}, "CUSTOMER_ID": {"column": "customer_id", "type": "NUM"}, "AMOUNT": {"column": "amount", "type": "NUM"}}},
 "CUSTOMERS": {"kind": "relation", "from": "customers", "alias": "c", "fields": {"ID": {"column": "id", "type": "NUM"}, "NAME": {"column": "name", "type": "TEXT"}}}}
--- as
statement
--- source
ORDERS .> LINK(CUSTOMERS, _1["CUSTOMER_ID"] == _2["ID"]) .> MAP(r, RECORD("n", r["NAME"]))
--- expect
SELECT `c`.`name` AS `n` FROM `orders` `o` INNER JOIN `customers` `c` ON (`o`.`customer_id` = `c`.`id`)
===

### name: link.binder.left-name-reads-the-left-relation
--- note
Review 2026-09-25 SQL-01. R and S share every field name, with different values
(in memory _["SS"]["VAL"] is R's). The left binder names the LEFT relation;
JS, PHP, Python and C++ resolved both explicit binders to the joined source and
selected s.val. Only same-named fields reveal it.
--- dialect
sqlite
--- bindings
{"R": {"kind": "relation", "from": "r", "alias": "r", "fields": {"ID": {"column": "id", "type": "NUM"}, "VAL": {"column": "val", "type": "NUM"}}},
 "S": {"kind": "relation", "from": "s", "alias": "s", "fields": {"ID": {"column": "id", "type": "NUM"}, "VAL": {"column": "val", "type": "NUM"}}}}
--- as
statement
--- source
R .> LINK(S, SS, X, SS["ID"] == X["ID"]) .> MAP(RECORD("picked", _["SS"]["VAL"]))
--- expect
SELECT "r"."val" AS "picked" FROM "r" "r" INNER JOIN "s" "s" ON (CAST("r"."id" AS NUMERIC) = CAST("s"."id" AS NUMERIC))
===
### name: link.binder.right-name-reads-the-right-relation
--- dialect
sqlite
--- bindings
{"R": {"kind": "relation", "from": "r", "alias": "r", "fields": {"ID": {"column": "id", "type": "NUM"}, "VAL": {"column": "val", "type": "NUM"}}},
 "S": {"kind": "relation", "from": "s", "alias": "s", "fields": {"ID": {"column": "id", "type": "NUM"}, "VAL": {"column": "val", "type": "NUM"}}}}
--- as
statement
--- source
R .> LINK(S, SS, X, SS["ID"] == X["ID"]) .> MAP(RECORD("picked", _["X"]["VAL"]))
--- expect
SELECT "s"."val" AS "picked" FROM "r" "r" INNER JOIN "s" "s" ON (CAST("r"."id" AS NUMERIC) = CAST("s"."id" AS NUMERIC))
===
### name: link.binder.both-names-in-one-projection
--- dialect
sqlite
--- bindings
{"R": {"kind": "relation", "from": "r", "alias": "r", "fields": {"ID": {"column": "id", "type": "NUM"}, "VAL": {"column": "val", "type": "NUM"}}},
 "S": {"kind": "relation", "from": "s", "alias": "s", "fields": {"ID": {"column": "id", "type": "NUM"}, "VAL": {"column": "val", "type": "NUM"}}}}
--- as
statement
--- source
R .> LINK(S, A, B, A["ID"] == B["ID"]) .> MAP(RECORD("l", _["A"]["VAL"], "r", _["B"]["VAL"]))
--- expect
SELECT "r"."val" AS "l", "s"."val" AS "r" FROM "r" "r" INNER JOIN "s" "s" ON (CAST("r"."id" AS NUMERIC) = CAST("s"."id" AS NUMERIC))
===
